n research hadn't really  thought through there was additional  risks  I might agree to them at the point that  the device might be surgically removed  and so important trade offs come up at  the end of a research study there are  ongoing risks if subjects decide to keep  a device implanted but there are also  risks to having the device removed and  so there's that that risk benefit  analysis really needs to take place on  an individual basis if a research  subject for instance has had a change in  their health status over the course of  participation in a research study and  then there might need to be a really  individualized analysis for whether it's  ethically and medically appropriate to  remove that that brain device especially  if that subject might be experiencing  benefit from the device and if the risks  have changed since they initially  enrolled in research so when we think  about research oversight and while IRB  approval might depend on minimizing  risks to subjects and and that may in  some ways push form I'm avoiding  unnecessary risks either related to  excellent of a device or continued use  of a device if there are ongoing risks  of infection let's say with a brain  implant it's important to note that  there there might be a gap in research  oversight once once the end of the trial  has has taken place because IRB might  have limited post trial oversight once a  research study ends another concern that  that I've come across in terms of the  thought I put into exit from research  protocols is that there seems to be  significant variability from one  clinical trial to another in terms of  what procedures there are for exit from  a clinical study so there doesn't seem  to be a very standard or consistent  approach to just explaining what options  research participants have at the end of  a clinical trial or the consent process  that might take place I mean also being  the analysis of the risks and benefits  of surgical removal at these  investigational devices  these inconsistent practices raise  ethical concerns related to justice and  I think it's important that researchers  share the practices that they use are  the options that they provide related to  surgical removal or post trial use of  implanted devices in part to address  these justice concerns we don't want  different benefits to accrued just to  some research participants where others  might might feel the sense that they're  forced to have a device that's  benefitting them removed  just by virtue of variation and clinical  trial design in an unanswered question  at this point comes up related to post  trial access and requirements for post  trial access we we still have not  clarified whether there's truly an  obligation to allow participants to  continue to use brain implants if  they're benefiting from that  investigational implant once they're  exiting from a research study in post  trial axis has been discussed quite a  bit initially it was a topic that  received some ethical interest during  HIV clinical trials of therapies and the  treatment of HIV and when we think about  brain devices its FDA regulation that  would generally apply that would be the  regulatory constraints for offering post  trial access to brand devices and some  continued access to brain and devices is  not FDA required but it is permitted  under expanded access provisions I mean  this this usually requires some  preliminary evidence that the device is  safe and effective and it does create  these ongoing obligations that I  mentioned before for safety monitoring  and adverse event reporting and so if an  investigator decides that they are going  to make sure that that participants in  their clinical trial have access to the  investigational device after the trial  is ended that creates and while I'm  doing responsibilities for that  investigator to monitor whether there  are any adverse events even after the  Nicolle trial has ended so their  multiple stakeholders when you just guys  post trial access to brain devices and  not includes participants in these  clinical trials the clinical researchers  themselves who may accrue additional  obligations if they decide to offer post  trial access this study sponsor may have  an important stake in where their  approach trial axis is offered because  this may really change the costs of  sponsoring research the FDA and other  regulatory bodies clearly have some some  stake in this since it may change the  amount of regulatory oversight that  needs to be provided research oversight  bodies such as IRB may have limited  jurisdiction when it comes to post trial  access or post trial device use but they  may have a stake in in they may have a  role at least in examining whether  there's a good enough plan for what  happens to investigational brain  implants after after a clinical trial  ends healthcare insurers may have a  stake in that if a research study  doesn't pay for follow-up care at that  follow-up care for research participants  may accrue to health care insurers  instead and I would say the public also  has a stake in these questions about  post royal access there there might be  concerns that people who participate in  research may have preferential access to  brain devices but also the public might  have an interest in encouraging people  to participate in research to contribute  to generalizable knowledge about health  conditions  I assume the public may actually have an  interest in offering post trial access  to the extent that it may be fair but  also at may incentivize research  participation so two scenarios are  important to think about when we when we  consider the ethical issues in post  trial access the first would be somebody  who benefits from a brain implant in a  clinical trial that's overall  unsuccessful so it's likely that that  brain device because the the clinical  trial is not successful that brain  device might not go to market  and so the costs and burdens of device  maintenance may be difficult to justify  for a sponsor but but the the benefit  that an individual who experienced the  risks or under underwent the risks of  participating in this research trial is  an important countervailing  consideration and finally as a second  scenario would be a a non responder in  an otherwise a successful clinical trial  and so that that non-responder may if  the device were to continue to be turned  on there's a possibility that that the  FATA non-responder will eventually  become a responder but may just have a  delayed response and so this brings up  questions about clinical trial design  and clinical endpoints that are chosen  in designing these clinical trials but  also this non-responder may be concerned  about the risks of device removal if it  seems like removing the device is the  best balance of risks and benefits for  that participant and it's also important  to consider the psychological impact it  can be incredibly devastating for  research process participants who have  have exposed themselves to significant  risks and invested quite a bit of time  and and hope in their participation in a  clinical trial and it can be devastating  to feel like even the last resort even  even this experimental treatment that  they tried when no FDA approved  treatments could help them have failed  and when we think about some of the  trade-offs and offering post trial  access and there are people who argue  that post trial access should be  mandated in some sense because it's it's  really impossible in an informed consent  process to help a research participant  understand what it might be like to have  access to your therapy that benefits  them and then to lose that access merely  because a clinical trial has ended  there's some people who say that the  post trial access is consistent with  some of the the entrustment or the  duties of care  that investigators assumed as they  engage in relationships investigator  subject relationships with with study  participants and there are also concerns  about just that just whether reciprocity  or concerns to avoid X like exploitation  really really create an obligation to  provide post-trial access but on the  other hand richard saber describes that  opposition to continued access its  investigational technologies might be  based on concerns about impeding  research prof progress but it may if you  create indefinite obligation for  researchers to provide continued access  to these investigational therapies that  that might disincentivize research it  may in fact incentivize participants to  withdraw early from a research study  their concerns about preferential access  and and also whether whether in fact  providing post oral access to you  investigational therapies could  exacerbate therapeutic misconception  could really exacerbate  misunderstandings people have about the  nature of research as opposed to  clinical care the Declaration of  Helsinki provides a little bit of  guidance about post trial access it  encourages provision of post trial  access to any intervention that's  identified as beneficial in the trial  although it doesn't provide all that  much clarity about about what is  considered benefit whether a study has  to show efficacy on a cohort basis or  just in an individual assessment of  whether an individual research  participant is experiencing benefit the  neuroethics roadmap that was recently  published by the NIH brain initiative  provides some additional guidance on on  both post trial access and some of the  earlier ethical issues I described when  we think about recruitment and informed  consent as well as exit from research so  the brain initiative the neuroethics  roadmap published by the brain  initiative describes that we need  to be attentive to you informed consent  processes we need to outline in detail  the potential end of trial and post  trial responsibilities that  investigators have to research  participants I mean really get get over  IRB oversight of these these plans and  they are particularly attentive to the  idea that we need to pay attention to  whether participants benefit from study  participation and how that benefit will  be managed in plans for for the end of a  clinical trial  um additionally they described that it's  important when a non clinical scientist  is designing or conducting a human human  subjects trial that their clinicians  included on the research team who are  sensitive to some of the clinical  concerns in clinical considerations that  arise in in conducting clinical research  they also describe that that it's  important to consider to anticipate in  advance potential psychosocial risks to  research participants so that might  include change and concerns about  changes and self-identity their  personality changes but also would  include what I mentioned about some of  the psychological risks of attempting to  participate in clinical research hoping  to experience benefit when when a person  has tried many other therapies that have  not provided benefit and finally the  neuroethics roadmap encourages  researchers to use real-world samples to  to include participants who have  coexisting or comorbid conditions in  order to make sure that that research  results in in generalizable findings and  this is these are good ethical practices  as well as just good research practices  and the the neuroethics roadmap  specifically describes some post study  responsibilities researchers should be  attentive to such as developing detailed  protocols about how they will handle  post study follow-up and paying  attention to the often overlooked  physical and psychological risks that  even a successful  intervention may pose to research  participants I described briefly some of  my own neuroethics  research and I was specifically  exploring some of these questions about  what what research participants oh sorry  what researchers oh research  participants at the end of clinical  trials of implanted brain devices and I  think it can be incredibly helpful to  integrate neuroethics research into  ongoing clinical research one can simply  get advice from an ethicist as they're  designing clinical trials especially in  early phase research one could  collaborate with an ethicist to to  explore some unique ethical issues so  having an ethics of study or an ethics  aim to an ongoing clinical research  proposal can help you to explore the  ethical concerns that might not be  immediately evident when when  considering designing an early phase  clinical trial and one can also conduct  parallel empirical ethics studies to  make sure that that we are working  closely together to understand some of  the ethical implications of early phase  clinical research and I think I'll  describe just a couple of ways that some  of the empirical neuroethics research  that's been funded by the brain  initiative and other areas of NIH other  Institute's within at NIH which I think  this research can help us understand  multiple stakeholder perspectives in  ways that might not be completely  apparent and when when overseeing  research empirical neuroethics research  can also can also help us understand  some overlooked ethical concerns as well  as provide some insight into just the  impact of research safeguards that are  already in place and an important  unanswered question that I'll end with  is I think I focused on some ethical  issues that arise once we're actually  implanting brain devices into humans I  think it's important to also think about  how these findings  can inform researchers when there  earlier along this translational  research process I so how how can the  work that we've done in neuroethics  inform just decisions about whether to  translate from a preclinical study idea  first in human study I said to me this  is the next question about how to make  sure that that ethics research and  findings from the ethics research that's  being conducted can actually support  researchers early along in the process  when these findings can be most  impactful down the road in translational  research that's what I will stop there I  hope to receive some questions from from  anyone who has questions after after  seeing this presentation and thank you  for your attention
 

